A||||9||danny boy||wheel size vs. unsprung weight||||1||
Z||000000||danny boy||05-26-2001||11:45 AM|||| The time has come where a wheel upgrade is needed. 17s look sooo good on the car, but 15s of the same wheel are gonna be lighter. Basically, since I don't expect anymore than 160 hp out of any engine that will ever be in MY car,I think my plan of attack will be to lighten the car as much as possible and go for handling excelence. I plan on going to a kosei or lenso wheel that is super light anyway, but the 17" version is 2lbs heavier than the 15" one. That is 8lbs total, which should make a difference. So, Is it worth sacrificing the big wheel looks for 2lbs/wheel?<P>------------------<BR>don't push the A/c button.. it catches the car on fire     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Master_Sushi||05-26-2001||01:26 PM||||I just got some nice 16s on my car, they are 16X7 sport edition valores from tirerack. I got some nice kumhos also, 225/50 on the back and 205-55 on the front. I noticed a HUGE improvement over my 14 inch tech wheels with snow tires  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. They look good, I would have got 17s but they would cost 200$ more all together if not more. But now that I've seen the 16s on the car I think they look about perfect. Ther were only $820 with everything. They kept my speedo accurate also.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||05-26-2001||03:53 PM||||Larger wheel size doesn't necessarily mean more weight. You have to think of the wheel tire combination.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||05-26-2001||05:39 PM||||If you're limited on power, don't think of unsprung weight, think of moment of inertia. A 17 inch wheel will always have a larger moment of inertia than a 15 inch wheel, unless the 15 is made of cast iron.<BR>If you don't care how your car looks, only how well it handles, get low profile tires for the lightest, widest 15" (or maybe 16") wheels you can find. The car will look ridiculous, but will handle well by virtue of having wide sticky tires, and because the smaller tire diameter will lower the car.<P>Thoroughly sorting out your suspension first will be cheaper than buying wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000004||danny boy||05-26-2001||11:36 PM||||suspension is pretty well good, I'm more worried about unsprung weight. I didn't think about the tire weight difference. 215/60-15 vs. 215/40-17. Hmmm.<P>------------------<BR>don't push the A/c button.. it catches the car on fire     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-27-2001||02:09 PM||||Some of the tire maker sites may give you a weight spec. Even if you use a diferant tire brand, the weight for a given size isn't likely to vary much.<P>Depends allot what you are using the car for and what you are diving on if the unsprung weight will have any real effect. Unsprung weight mainly becomes an issue on rough roads. <P>Inertia on the other hand effects acceration etc at all times. A few pounds one way or the other isn't going to be much issue in most cases. You'd only be able to measure such an effect on a computer timed track most of the time. You're looking at 100ths to 10ths of a second even then...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dave Gunsul||05-27-2001||03:18 PM||||Every little bit helps!!<BR>I put some Kosi K1 Racing rims on my GT and noticed a difference in acceleration.<BR>The original GT rims weigh between 18 and 22<BR>pounds and the Kosi's weigh 13.5 IB's so this<BR>is only a 18 IB difference and yet I still noticed quicker acceleration and better handling! Many people just don't believe that<BR>weight can make a difference but I can tell you from personal experience that it DOES!<BR>(my Kosi wheels are 15x7's BTW)<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000007||danny boy||05-28-2001||01:20 PM|||| Those were the wheels I was looking at. Have a pic of them on the car? Tire rack uses a coupe for everything. <BR> Since I have new tires on my GT wheels I think I'm going with the 15s.||||reg||1
